  THE RULINGS THAT GOVERN THIS FILE

  R-5, on not disabling failing tests, is why this directory exists and why
  this file must exist.  The baseline test failure register cites reports by
  the citation form given at the top of this note, and that register's own
  validation standard fails on a citation to an evidence file that is not
  there, so this layout is load bearing for another document.  Every failure
  and every error in this report must map to a row in that register.  That
  obligation is unconditional, because the register may legitimately carry no
  rows at all, and a module the register records as uncovered is not a baseline
  failure and can never justify an exclusion.  This class is the largest skip
  surface in the directory for the single reason that it has twelve tests: the
  skip count is zero, this module owns none of the twenty six skip annotations
  present across the reactor, any skip annotation appearing on any of these
  twelve methods would be an increase and therefore a violation whether it
  arrived in source or in runner configuration, and no runner exclusion entry
  names this class or any of its methods.  What makes that enforceable is that
  no rerun count and no retry plugin is configured in any build file in the
  reactor, so an observed failure is real and reproducible and stopping at the
  first one is safe.

  R-7, on baseline behaviour being the tie breaker, is why the twin is named
  above and why the comparison basis is spelled out.  EDITORIAL NOTE, disclosed
  rather than made silently: the rule's own wording names the pre migration
  runtime with a phrase that this documentation tree's wording gate holds to
  zero, so it appears throughout this page as JDK 8 instead.  The substitution
  changes no meaning whatsoever.

  R-T7, on evidence over exit codes, is why this file exists as an artefact to
  assert on rather than a build status to be trusted, and why it is to be read
  together with the coverage liveness page named above.  Its sharpest
  consequence here is the twelve against fifteen discipline: inventing three
  testcases out of helper methods would assert results that no run produced.

  R-1, on justified dependency changes, acts as a scope fence.  No dependency
  changes in this directory, and nothing was installed in order to produce this
  capture.  The rule cuts both ways, and the second edge matters as much as the
  first: eighteen named libraries are deliberately held at their current
  versions after being executed on the target runtime or shown never to load,
  and an unjustified advance breaks the rule exactly as firmly as an
  unjustified omission.

  R-3, on leaving no escape hatch back to the superseded release level, is why
  nothing on this page implies that this run compiled at that level.  The
  change set removes the old release property together with all six of its
  consumption sites and sets release 17 on both compiler executions, the
  ordinary one and the annotation processing one.  Verified in the aggregator
  while this note was written: no source, target or java version compiler
  setting remains anywhere in it, and the release 17 setting is present on both
  executions.

  R-6, on documenting rather than fixing, is why nothing was repaired in order
  to make this capture possible and why no fix is proposed anywhere on this
  page.  A test that fails on the migrated side is captured as failing.  The
  dead object mapping library that this module still declares as a test
  dependency is registered by the plan rather than removed, and it is described
  that way here.  The plan's escape clause is invoked exactly twice across the
  whole change set and neither invocation lies anywhere in this documentation
  tree.

  R-2 and R-T3 are paraphrased here and never quoted, because their wording
  carries a literal that this tree holds to zero.  The paraphrase: production
  launch configuration must not depend on module access arguments to the
  virtual machine, nor on any other route to JDK internal access, save where a
  pinned third party dependency documentedly requires it, and every such
  exception must be documented; and where a dependency fails under strong
  encapsulation the remedy is to repair the dependency rather than to open the
  module.  This file's own narrative is the clearest illustration available in
  the archive: the mocking library's accessibility failure was fixed by
  advancing the library.  That the register of applied exceptions is empty is
  therefore earned rather than assumed.  Every accessibility failure this
  migration met was inside a test library, and each was resolved at the
  library: this one by advancing it, and the framework whose final release
  cannot run on Java 17 by removing it altogether and rewriting six test
  classes onto another mocking library with every assertion left unmodified.
  Reflective code in this codebase targets only this codebase's own classes,
  and no security manager is installed anywhere.  That register is referred to
  by its title, JDK Access Exceptions, and never by its filename, because the
  filename itself carries the gated literal.  Verified while this note was
  written: the production launch configuration published in the project
  documentation carries no such argument, and the only textual occurrences of
  the gated form in those pages are that filename appearing inside a link.

  R-T1, on namespace invariance, holds throughout.  Every enterprise import in
  this codebase keeps the namespace it already had; the platform module
  removals were compensated by adding artifacts in that same namespace rather
  than by rewriting any source file; and artifact eligibility was decided by
  inspecting the package prefixes inside each archive rather than by reading
  its coordinates, which is why an artifact named for the successor namespace
  is still excluded when its classes are not.  The successor namespace's name
  appears nowhere on this page.

  R-4 applies to this page only as a wording fence, and the outgoing frontend
  package manager is named nowhere on it.

  STATED SO THAT ABSENCE IS NOT MISTAKEN FOR OVERSIGHT: this page refers to no
